WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.878
-As a magician... wizard...
-As a, as a wizard.

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:05.214
I felt like I could honestly
see dragons.

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:08.967
Life gives you lemonades.
No. Is that right?

00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:10.552
-Lemons give you lemon--
-Lemons.

00:00:10.636 --> 00:00:11.470
What is it?

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:21.772
-Hello. I'm George Robinson.
-I am Chinenye Ezeudu.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.941
-I'm Ncuti.
-And I'm Kedar, and today...

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.193
We're gonna be making
a perfect cup of tea...

00:00:26.276 --> 00:00:28.737
Whilst also talking
about identity.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.655
There's nothing better

00:00:29.738 --> 00:00:30.906
than a chat
over a cup of tea,

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:32.115
-is there?
-Trust.

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:33.700
Should we spill the tea?

00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:35.536
Let's get stirring.

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:43.752
Ncuti Gatwa
is a Scottish-Rwandan actor

00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:46.088
who's got
a lot of love to give,

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:49.424
but also is still
figuring things out.

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:51.385
How'd you describe yourself
in a sentence?

00:00:51.468 --> 00:00:52.302
I'd say

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:54.304
Kedar Williams-Stirling
is still figuring it out.

00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:55.347
Twenty six, bruv.

00:00:56.557 --> 00:00:58.141
I've only just started.

00:01:00.686 --> 00:01:02.271
I'm 45, man.

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:04.606
Don't you talk to me
about 26.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.358
How would you describe me

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:08.235
and I'll do
how I would describe you.

00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:09.987
Energetic, kind,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.239
and bring a fantastic energy
into any room.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:16.285
I would describe you
as being really funny

00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:18.203
and nice to be around

00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:20.789
and you're really talented
as well.

00:01:20.873 --> 00:01:23.083
Look at you!
You're, like, cringing.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:24.668
But it's all true.

00:01:28.213 --> 00:01:29.840
The first word
that comes to my mind

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.425
is parents.
I don't know why.

00:01:32.384 --> 00:01:33.302
Interesting.

00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:36.305
And you've been growing up,
grown up in a few places,

00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:38.599
so I guess that is quite
a rich upbringing

00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:39.516
within itself.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.895
Self is the first word
I think about

00:01:42.978 --> 00:01:44.271
when I think about identity.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.398
How you are as a person.

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:48.942
What you believe in,
what you stand for.

00:01:49.026 --> 00:01:50.819
It's in The Matrix,
isn't it?

00:01:50.903 --> 00:01:53.322
That idea
of residual self-image

00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:55.908
of how
you perceive yourself.

00:01:55.991 --> 00:01:59.494
I think it's simultaneously
what you think of yourself

00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:01.872
inside yourself, but also,

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.291
how you present yourself
to others,

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:05.584
-if that make sense?
-Yeah.

00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:12.466
Family. Family influence.
Mean a lot.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:14.510
The experiences
that we go through

00:02:14.593 --> 00:02:16.720
-shape our perspectives.
-Yeah.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.013
Even listening to music.

00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:20.140
I'm a completely
different person

00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:21.558
when I'm listening
to pop music.

00:02:21.642 --> 00:02:23.060
-I'm more fun.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.770
And when I'm listening
to rock music

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:26.063
I'm a different breed.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.066
But those kind of things
just shape you.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.692
And I think,
a lot of the times,

00:02:30.776 --> 00:02:34.154
is the way that we try
and negotiate the world.

00:02:34.738 --> 00:02:37.991
We build mechanisms
within ourselves

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:39.618
to tackle
certain situations.

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:41.912
We are the sum
of our experiences.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:46.500
My parents, friends,
books, films, traveling.

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:47.876
The Harry Potter books,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.002
-right?
-Fair.

00:02:49.086 --> 00:02:51.380
Like, really,
really formed my identity

00:02:51.463 --> 00:02:52.923
-as a little kid.
-As a magician.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.883
-As a, as a wizard!
-Wizard.

00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:57.427
I was just so obsessed
with anything fantastical.

00:02:57.511 --> 00:02:59.721
Like that was on my mind,
was on when I was a kid.

00:02:59.805 --> 00:03:03.517
Like, I felt like I could
honestly see dragons.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.060
Really?
I hear it, I hear it.

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:06.812
So,
I just loved Harry Potter,

00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:08.188
like anything fantastical.

00:03:13.277 --> 00:03:15.362
Yeah. Especially with
our characters.

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:17.656
People expect your character
to be a dick, right?

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:18.907
But then you're just not.

00:03:18.991 --> 00:03:20.993
Like, we're just playing
these characters

00:03:21.076 --> 00:03:23.370
and playing these people
and we're just not them.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:25.414
There's definitely
a conflict in terms of

00:03:25.497 --> 00:03:27.249
for a character you'll use

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:29.376
a set of traits
that you have yourself

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:33.172
or there just naturally is
some overlap,

00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:35.757
but takes real courage
is to push against that

00:03:35.841 --> 00:03:37.551
and just be like,
no, no, no, I'm me.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:39.678
I feel
it's hard to be yourself

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:41.180
in this day and age

00:03:41.263 --> 00:03:44.516
with, like,
Instagram, Twitter,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.728
people dictating what
the beauty standards are

00:03:47.811 --> 00:03:49.938
and you just really
have to home in on yourself

00:03:50.022 --> 00:03:52.149
-and be like, who am I?
-Yeah.

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.150
And that's, I think,

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:56.778
the hardest person
to be with is yourself.

00:03:56.862 --> 00:03:59.239
Yeah. I think, obviously,
with my experience

00:03:59.323 --> 00:04:03.535
I've lot of time
to think about who I am,

00:04:03.619 --> 00:04:07.164
and what it means
to be George Robinson,

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:09.917
and not allow

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.504
other people's perceptions
of me affect how I act.

00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:15.005
Think Steven Fry
said something

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:16.548
really interesting
to me on set.

00:04:16.632 --> 00:04:18.509
He was like,
"I am not one thing."

00:04:18.592 --> 00:04:21.386
"I am not just an actor,
I am all things."

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:28.894
I think there's a narrative
that's put on to us

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:32.022
as black males
and a sort of responsibility

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.774
that you don't
I don't even think realise

00:04:33.857 --> 00:04:35.067
until you're a certain age

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.568
and you're aware
of more things

00:04:36.652 --> 00:04:39.404
that weren't even
in your mindset

00:04:39.488 --> 00:04:42.366
that you then kind of feel
part of in a way

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:44.868
that is actually not
anything to do with you...

00:04:44.952 --> 00:04:46.703
-Right. Right.
-...as a being.

00:04:46.787 --> 00:04:48.455
But yet, you're universal.

00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:49.915
Your experience
in this world

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:51.792
is shaped
by this external thing,

00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:53.293
which, of course,
then is part of you.

00:04:53.377 --> 00:04:54.211
Because you are

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.546
experiencing this world
as a black person.

00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:56.922
And then, I guess, also,

00:04:57.005 --> 00:05:00.884
nobody ever knows you
really fully.

00:05:00.968 --> 00:05:02.219
I don't really feel like

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:03.887
you ever
fully know yourself.

00:05:03.971 --> 00:05:05.681
You're constantly learning

00:05:05.764 --> 00:05:07.349
and constantly
experiencing things.

00:05:12.271 --> 00:05:13.730
Think you co-switch
subconsciously.

00:05:13.814 --> 00:05:15.190
For example, like Mumsy.

00:05:15.274 --> 00:05:16.525
Me and Mum
have a great relationship,

00:05:16.608 --> 00:05:17.693
but there's
a transparency there

00:05:17.776 --> 00:05:20.153
that is still different
to my bedroom,

00:05:20.237 --> 00:05:21.405
-know what I mean?
-Right.

00:05:21.488 --> 00:05:22.948
It's not even
a conscious thing,

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:24.825
it's like a subconscious.
Just way of...

00:05:24.908 --> 00:05:26.326
don't know
if that's a bad thing.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:27.244
So therefore,

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:28.787
you saying about
the middle-aged white guy

00:05:28.871 --> 00:05:30.497
who might be going
for whatever prejudices

00:05:30.581 --> 00:05:32.916
and bestows them on to you
and then me coming to you

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.334
and chatting to you
about something.

00:05:34.418 --> 00:05:35.878
You co-switching
between the two

00:05:35.961 --> 00:05:37.880
is you just
kind of navigating

00:05:37.963 --> 00:05:40.465
how to just be
in that space.

00:05:40.549 --> 00:05:42.176
I don't know
if that is a bad thing.

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:44.094
'Cause I think, as humans,
we do that naturally

00:05:44.178 --> 00:05:45.804
'cause that is just
what humans are.

00:05:45.888 --> 00:05:48.015
We adapt and evolve.

00:05:48.098 --> 00:05:49.016
Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:51.894 --> 00:05:53.604
Just keep on brewing.

00:05:54.313 --> 00:05:56.565
I love you so much.
You are so brilliant.

00:05:56.648 --> 00:05:58.025
I love you so much.

00:05:58.108 --> 00:05:59.610
I would say be strong,

00:05:59.693 --> 00:06:04.740
wait for life to happen
and the colours will change.

00:06:04.823 --> 00:06:08.243
And you'll get stronger
as time goes by.

00:06:09.286 --> 00:06:10.245
The stronger you are,

00:06:10.329 --> 00:06:12.497
the more depth of flavour
you'll have.

00:06:12.581 --> 00:06:14.458
If you want some acidity,

00:06:14.541 --> 00:06:16.043
-go a bit of lemons...
-Yeah.

00:06:16.126 --> 00:06:18.337
-Or ginger. Do that.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.421
-Chuck it in.
-Exactly.

00:06:19.505 --> 00:06:21.423
Try experiment new things.

00:06:21.507 --> 00:06:25.260
Life gives you lemonades.
No. Is that right?

00:06:25.344 --> 00:06:26.803
-Lemons give you lemon--
-Lemons.

00:06:26.887 --> 00:06:27.721
What is it?

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:31.391
You're a cup of tea, man.

00:06:31.475 --> 00:06:32.643
Just enjoy it, man.

